let's talk about the war in ukraine and  its impact on the people there  in just three weeks life for millions of  people has become unrecognizable  [Music]  but with so much going on and so many  distressing pictures and stories  flooding our screens it can be hard to  keep track of what's happening where so  let's run you through it  we're going to start with some numbers  that help to show the scale of all of  this  now the u.n has confirmed 691 civilian  deaths but says the real number is  likely much higher and officials from  the city of mariupol alone say 2  500 residents have been killed  ukraine's got a population of 44 million  people around 2 million of them have  left their homes and fled to other parts  of the country mostly to the west  more than 3 million have left ukraine  altogether and crossed into neighboring  countries like poland romania and  moldova  most of them are women and children  because men of military age those  between 18 and 60 are banned from  leaving  them  mother  um  we lost our life  our safety  we can get that plan any future because  we don't know what will be tomorrow  the families i've met here along the  border um they tell me that our plan  basically stops here our plan was to get  to safety and now we have no idea where  we're going next we need shelter we need  an income we need money we need to  survive when it comes to what people  still inside ukraine are dealing with  well it varies depending on where they  are  so far the war has mostly been  concentrated in the north the east and  parts of the south  you can see the areas that russia now  controls and where its forces are  operating  hereson was the first major town they've  managed to take control of  there have been big protests there  against the occupation but also reports  that food and other supplies are running  low and people aren't able to leave  then there are several other towns and  cities that are still holding out  against russian forces but at an  enormous cost  like kharkiv ukraine's second largest  city where entire neighborhoods now lie  in ruins this street in central kharkiv  shows you just how devastating  the effect of russian bombing of this  city has been  an entire street  totally destroyed  i know you can use deep brush  here  [Music]  hundreds of thousands of people did get  out but around half a million are still  there and the shelling hasn't stopped  in the north is another place where  things are really bad and residential  areas are also being bombed  one attack hit a square where people  were lining up for bread local officials  say 47 people died those who are still  living in these besieged areas where  there is intensive you know shelling  fighting going on daily life is very  much about sheltering in a basement and  trying to survive on the food the water  the medicines that you managed to kind  of stock up on before it started the big  problem right now  is to bring the food from where it is to  where it has to go into these cities the  supply chains are completely disrupted  you know bridges have been bombed um um  you know supermarkets are empty trains  you know have been destroyed trucks have  been destroyed drivers are worried to  drive to these places one of the places  people are particularly worried about is  mario pol which has been almost totally  cut off with around 400 000 trapped  there it's surrounded by russian forces  and for the last two weeks they've been  attacking relentlessly  this is where there was an attack on a  maternity hospital which ukraine's  president has called a war crime  there are reports of bodies lying in the  streets and the authorities have  resorted to mass graves to bury the dead  there's no running water  food and medicine are running out  shops and pharmacies have been looted  communications are down there's no  electricity and it's freezing cold  there have been attempts to get a  ceasefire in place so that people can  get out and aid can get into mariopol  but they haven't really worked  so far only 2 000 cars have been able to  escape  another 2 000 are still waiting to leave  according to city officials  meanwhile the rest of the country is  watching what's going on in places like  mariupol and kharkiv and they're bracing  for the worst too especially in the  capital kiev where russian forces are  closing in and the fight is getting  closer  [Music]  now that's the entrance of a residential  building here in the north of kiev  and just take a look at the damage you  can still actually smell the burning  the center of the city has been heavily  fortified  shops are mainly closed  half of the city's population is left  according to the mayor  and those who have stayed are mostly  sheltering in places like basements and  metro stations  for those who remain and kiev life has  moved deep on the ground here they hope  to be safe from russian bombs and  missiles but they're all holding their  breath for what's coming next  okay now let's look at what's going on  in the west of ukraine there's no big  presence of russian forces there  although there have been some attacks  mostly targeting military facilities  levieve is the main city in the west and  it's still functioning relatively  normally there's a nighttime curfew but  during the day people are out on the  streets and going to work  livia has also become a hub for ngos and  a transit point for supplies coming into  the country in western parts of ukraine  everything is more or less okay and you  can buy all necessary things in the  supermarket or shops and actually what  we are doing right now here in even our  cities we are buying and getting  everything what we can get and trying to  send it to kiev to other cities  especially  in  eastern part of ukraine but leve is also  dealing with the arrival of hundreds of  thousands of people who fled from other  parts of the country it's a huge  displacement crisis people there have  two basic strategies the first one is to  stay in a relatively stable part of the  ukraine or  they stay just for two days and continue  further west to slovakia or poland even  if western ukraine is relatively stable  now  will that last there is certainly a  growing sense of unease here in the city  of levith because in previous nights  there were air raid sirens which  sometimes lasted as long as three hours  and we know that those sirens are linked  to the increased number of russian  attacks on targets in western ukraine in  each discussion i had here  during the last  10 days  there was just one word we want peace we  want peace to come home  because we want  to live our normal lives as we did  before this russian invasion  if you missed it check out our last  episode about why nato is ruling out a  no-fly zone for ukraine click the link  here to watch and don't forget to  subscribe to this youtube channel so you  don't miss our upcoming episodes  [Music]
 

